  "content": {
    "id": "p25-q14-root",
    "type": "question",
    "label": "14",
    "prompt": "Give an example of a surjective function $$f:(0,1)\\to[0,1].$$",
    "layout": "list",
    "columns": null,
    "questionDiagrams": [],
    "children": [],
    "answer": {
      "short": "One example is $$f(x)=\\begin{cases}1,&0<x\\le\\frac14,\\\\\\frac32-2x,&\\frac14<x<\\frac34,\\\\0,&\\frac34\\le x<1.\\end{cases}$$\n\nThe middle branch gives every value between $0$ and $1$, and the constant branches supply both endpoints.",
      "worked": "Define $$f(x)=\\begin{cases}1,&0<x\\le\\frac14,\\\\\\frac32-2x,&\\frac14<x<\\frac34,\\\\0,&\\frac34\\le x<1.\\end{cases}$$ All its outputs lie in $[0,1]$.\n\nFor any $0<y<1$, choose $x=\\frac34-\\frac y2$. Then $\\frac14<x<\\frac34$ and $$\\begin{align*}f(x)&=\\frac32-2\\left(\\frac34-\\frac y2\\right)\\\\f(x)&=y.\\end{align*}$$ Also, $f(\\frac14)=1$ and $f(\\frac34)=0$. Thus every element of $[0,1]$ has a preimage in $(0,1)$, so $f$ is surjective.",
      "solutionDiagrams": []
    },
    "answerSpaceMm": 52
  },
